      "createdAt": 1793232000000,
      "updatedAt": 1793232000000,
      "id": "ivanhoe-source-17-p25",
      "chapterId": "ivanhoe-section-17",
      "timelineId": "ivanhoe-reading-order",
      "title": "The courtiers’ raillery",
      "description": "Mocks the Saxons’ pedigrees and cloaks, then enjoys Cedric’s counterstroke. Adds a jest that Saxons lead as deer before dogs. Mocks Saxon manners under the form of praise. Adds mock praise of temperance and dismisses fear of Saxon resistance. Taunts Saxon conduct at Hastings. Condemns the abuse of hospitality and reminds the defeated knights of their falls.",
      "locationMarkerId": "ivanhoe-place-ashby-castle-hall",
      "involvedCharacterIds": [
        "ivanhoe-character-john",
        "ivanhoe-character-malvoisin",
        "ivanhoe-character-aymer",
